<p><strong>Update 9 Oct 2026 9:41 PM</strong><br/>
Nathan Grigg, who has set me straight too many times to count, <a href="https://mastodon.social/@nathangrigg/117413516387160372">pointed out</a> that the <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.sum.html">NumPy docs for <code>sum</code></a> explain how that function uses algorithms with different precision depending on</p>
<ol>
<li>whether the <code>axis</code> parameter is used or not; and</li>
<li>which axis is chosen if the parameter is used.</li>
</ol>
<p>There’s basically a speed/precision tradeoff at work. While it’s true that <a href="https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.mean.html">the docs for <code>mean</code></a> don’t include this explanation, <code>sum</code> and <code>mean</code> are so alike that it must be the reason for the different answers.</p>
<p>Thanks, Nathan!</p>
